# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Compression layer: Section 3 (Introduction). Overrides pass-1 text where keyed.
# expect prefix must match the text in place AFTER the rev layer (pass-1 text if
# that key was revised there, otherwise the original dump text).

REVS = {
    "P36": ("South Africa generates most of its electricity from coal,",
            "South Africa generates most of its electricity from coal, and the costs of that dependence reach households directly. Scheduled load-shedding reached record levels in 2023 and has since been suspended for long periods, but the system remains vulnerable to breakdowns, and the price of electricity has risen faster than household incomes. For a middle-income household these are costs to be managed. For a household in an informal settlement they determine whether there is a hot meal, whether a child can study after dark, and whether the money set aside for electricity lasts the month."),
    "P37": ("The aggregate picture looks better than the household one.",
            "The aggregate picture looks better than the household one. Around nine in ten South African households now have a grid connection (Statistics South Africa, 2023), yet connection is not the same as access. A connected household may still be unable to afford enough electricity, may receive an intermittent supply, may live in a dwelling whose wiring cannot carry a cooking load, or may buy through a landlord's meter at a marked-up price. Energy poverty in South Africa is therefore less a problem of missing infrastructure than of how infrastructure, income, tariffs, dwelling conditions and household practice fit together. That distinction shifts attention from the presence of a technology to the arrangement in which it works, or fails to work."),
    "P38": ("Policy has begun to reflect this.",
            "Policy has begun to reflect this. The Presidential Climate Commission's just transition framework accepts that decarbonisation and social equity cannot be separated (Presidential Climate Commission, 2022), and the Integrated Resource Plan sets out a shift away from coal alongside a commitment to security of supply (Department of Mineral Resources and Energy, 2019). The Just Energy Transition Partnership has added international finance and, with it, expectations of procedural fairness. Recent research shows, however, that the obstacles are as much institutional and social as financial or technical (Molepo et al., 2025; Mirzania et al., 2023): municipalities lack the capacity and revenue to fulfil their distribution duties, host communities have rarely been consulted meaningfully, and the households least able to pay are the least able to wait for benefits planned at national scale."),
    "P40": ("Informal settlements are not a single kind of place.",
            "Informal settlements are not a single kind of place. They differ in age, size, location, tenure and access to services, and some have been upgraded while others rely on shared taps and informal connections. What they share is insecure tenure, self-built housing and incomplete municipal recognition, and that combination shapes both what is technically possible and who is able to make decisions about the household."),
    "P41": ("Three features are particularly relevant here.",
            "Three features are particularly relevant. The first is density: dwellings are close together, access routes are narrow and space for equipment is limited, so a technology that needs a dedicated yard, a water supply or a stable foundation may not fit. The second is the landlord-tenant relationship: many households rent a room or shack on someone else's plot, the meter may be in the landlord's name, and the tenant may have little say over wiring or appliances. The third is fuel stacking. Households rarely use one fuel for everything: electricity may serve lighting and phone charging while paraffin, firewood, charcoal or LPG serve cooking and heating. The choice is not habit alone; it reflects cost per unit of useful energy, reliability, pot size, weather, availability and the taste of the food (Musango, 2022; Musango et al., 2020)."),
    "P42": ("Fuel stacking has consequences that a simple access metric cannot capture.",
            "Fuel stacking has consequences that a simple access metric cannot capture. Indoor smoke from solid fuels remains a significant health risk, particularly for women and young children; paraffin is a common cause of fires and of accidental ingestion; collecting wood takes time, usually from women; and a household that has \u201caccess\u201d may still keep a paraffin stove for the evenings when the power is off. These are the substance of what an energy service is, and they explain why a technology that performs well in a laboratory can perform poorly in a settlement."),
    "P44": ("The Living Lab has become a common way of organising innovation",
            "The Living Lab has become a common way of organising innovation in real-world settings: users, researchers, firms and public bodies co-create, prototype and test solutions in the context where they will be used (Bergvall-K\u00e5reborn and St\u00e5hlbr\u00f6st, 2009; Leminen et al., 2012). Applied in information technology, health and urban planning, the concept has more recently entered energy, where a systematic review found it used in several different ways \u2014 as a space, a methodology, an organisation or a network \u2014 and few applications in African informal settlements, with thin evidence on outcomes (Mbatha and Musango, 2022)."),
    "P45": ("The South African research programme on gendered energy innovation",
            "The South African research programme on gendered energy innovation is the most direct precedent. Its products include a gender analysis framework (Anditi et al., 2022), a systems model of energy service security (Batinge et al., 2022), a multi-stakeholder co-design toolkit (Petrulaityte et al., 2022) and a process for setting up energy Living Labs (Mukama et al., 2022). The programme established that gender is not an add-on to energy innovation and that service security depends on social as well as technical arrangements, and it supplies a practical vocabulary for working with communities. This study builds on that programme rather than claiming to start from nothing."),
    "P46": ("Biogas is selected from several candidate decentralised technologies",
            "Biogas is selected from several candidate decentralised technologies for three reasons. First, anaerobic digestion converts organic waste into a gas for cooking, the service electricity most often fails to provide in informal settlements. Second, the technology makes the socio-material arrangement visible: a digester needs feedstock, water, space, stable temperature, regular attention and a route for the digestate, each connecting the artefact to the household, the neighbourhood, the municipality and the waste stream. Third, community-scale biogas has a mixed record in southern Africa \u2014 some installations work for years, many fail quickly through maintenance, feedstock, water or responsibility problems \u2014 and that variation is exactly what a study of acceptance needs."),
    "P47": ("Biogas is not presented here as a universal solution;",
            "Biogas is not presented as a universal solution; it is a test case. If the framework can explain why a biogas system is taken up in one part of a settlement and abandoned in another, it should transfer to other decentralised technologies, including solar water heating, improved cookstoves and small-scale PV."),
    "P50": ("The discipline of Engineering Management has tools for these questions:",
            "Engineering Management has tools for such questions \u2014 requirements analysis, systems modelling, project and lifecycle management, risk assessment \u2014 but it lacks a theoretical account of the relationship between a technology and the social arrangement into which it is introduced. Without that account, the tools are applied to the artefact and the arrangement is left to chance. This study argues that the arrangement is the proper object of design, and that a theory is needed to describe it."),
    "P52": ("Energy interventions in South African informal settlements tend to follow a similar pattern.",
            "Energy interventions in South African informal settlements tend to follow a familiar pattern. A technology is selected, a pilot is funded, equipment is installed and training provided. For a time it works. Then a part fails and cannot be sourced, feedstock becomes expensive, the maintainer moves away, the landlord raises the rent, or the promised service does not arrive. Use declines, the equipment is abandoned, and the project is recorded as a technical success that did not achieve its social objectives."),
    "P53": ("There are three explanations for this pattern, and this study takes all three seriously.",
            "Three explanations for this pattern are taken seriously here. The first is analytical. The models that dominate applied energy research treat acceptance as an individual decision about a finished artefact: the Technology Acceptance Model and its successors ask whether a person finds a technology useful and easy to use (Davis, 1989; Venkatesh et al., 2003). Such questions can be answered in a survey, but they cannot explain why the same technology is used in one street and abandoned in the next. They abstract away the things that matter: the cost of fuel, the reliability of supply, the space available, the division of labour in the household, and the terms on which the dwelling is occupied."),
    "P54": ("The second explanation is methodological.",
            "The second explanation is methodological. Participation is now a standard requirement in energy projects, but it is often consultative rather than consequential: residents are invited to a meeting, their views are recorded, and the design is finalised elsewhere. This can legitimise a decision already taken, raise expectations the project cannot meet, and advantage those already comfortable speaking in public. The participation literature has described these failure modes for decades (Arnstein, 1969), yet they recur because the incentives to consult are stronger than the incentives to share control."),
    "P55": ("The third explanation is evaluative.",
            "The third explanation is evaluative. Projects are usually judged by outputs \u2014 connections made, capacity installed, people trained \u2014 which say little about whether the intervention was just. A project can connect a settlement while the poorest pay more per unit than the wealthy, reduce smoke while transferring the burden of collecting feedstock to girls, or create a community asset that a local elite captures. Without criteria that make these distributions visible, an evaluation can report success while the injustice persists."),
    "P67": ("The study is guided by five research questions.",
            "The study is guided by five research questions, following the questions set out in the research brief with minor editorial changes for clarity. They are deliberately open \u2014 they ask how and which, not whether a single variable has an effect \u2014 because the study is concerned with mechanisms and configurations rather than with estimating causal parameters across a population."),
    "P75": ("The relationship between the questions is cumulative rather than parallel.",
            "The relationship between the questions is cumulative rather than parallel. RQ1 establishes the conceptual frame; RQ2 and RQ3 populate it with evidence about participation and acceptance; RQ4 turns that evidence into a design mechanism; and RQ5 tests whether the mechanism holds together in practice. The sequence matters because a failure at RQ1 cannot be repaired by better data at RQ4."),
    "P78": ("The study addresses a gap at the intersection of four literatures:",
            "The study addresses a gap at the intersection of four literatures: energy justice, Living Lab methodology, socio-technical systems thinking and critical posthumanism. Each is well developed in its own field yet falls short of this problem. Energy justice has a robust normative vocabulary but says little about how an engineer should design a prototype; Living Lab research describes co-creation in detail but evaluates outputs rather than justice; systems thinking maps complexity but often remains functionalist; and posthumanism offers a sophisticated ontology of human\u2013technology relations that has rarely been applied to energy infrastructure in the Global South."),
    "P79": ("The contribution is not the invention of a new theory.",
            "The contribution is not the invention of a new theory but the construction of a defensible bridge between these bodies of work, tested against a case. Bridge-building of this kind is a legitimate scholarly contribution, but each tradition must be represented on its own terms, the points of incompatibility stated rather than smoothed over, and the resulting framework must generate propositions that could fail."),
    "P81": ("For practitioners, the study offers three things.",
            "For practitioners, the study offers a design protocol that treats the socio-material configuration, rather than the artefact, as the unit of intervention; evaluation criteria that make distributional and procedural questions visible alongside technical performance; and evidence about what happens when a community-scale biogas system is co-designed in an informal settlement, including the conditions under which it is likely to be maintained. These are modest outputs, but they are the kind that project managers and municipal officials can use."),
    "P83": ("At the policy level, the study speaks to the implementation",
            "At the policy level, the study speaks to the implementation of the just transition framework at municipal scale. Municipalities are responsible for distribution, integrated development planning and, in many cases, for settlements that national policy treats as temporary. The study's attention to procedural justice, to the terms on which residents are included, and to how costs and benefits are distributed is directly relevant to the design of municipal energy programmes and the evaluation of JETP-funded projects."),
    "P85": ("The study contributes a methodological approach",
            "The study contributes a methodological approach combining reflexive thematic analysis, situational mapping, causal loop diagramming and diffractive reading. Each method is established, but they are rarely used together, and the epistemological work of combining them has not been done. The study will document how the combination was applied, what it produced that a single method would have missed, and where it created tensions \u2014 a documentation that is itself a contribution to qualitative energy research."),
    "P87": ("The study is deliberately bounded.",
            "The study is deliberately bounded. It examines one informal settlement in Greater Tzaneen, Limpopo Province, over three years, focusing on household cooking and a community-scale biogas system. It does not model the national electricity system, evaluate the life-cycle impacts of biogas, or estimate willingness to pay across a representative sample; those questions require different designs and resources."),
    "P88": ("The study is a proposal.",
            "The study is a proposal. No fieldwork has been conducted and no empirical data exist. The methods described in Section 7 are intended methods, to be implemented only after ethical clearance and after agreements with the municipality, community leadership and the households involved."),
    "P89": ("Four further delimitations should be stated.",
            "Four further delimitations should be stated. First, the study does not claim biogas is the best technology for the site, only that it is a useful case for examining the framework. Second, it aims for analytical rather than statistical generalisation, in the sense described by Yin (2009) and defended by Flyvbjerg (2006). Third, it records reported symptoms and observed conditions rather than clinical measures of indoor air pollution, treating them as one dimension of wellbeing. Fourth, it does not treat \u201cthe community\u201d as a unified actor: it deliberately samples for difference, including tenants, landlords, women's groups, youth and municipal officials."),
    "P95": ("The proposal follows the twelve-section structure required by the Postgraduate School",
            "The proposal follows the required twelve-section structure. Section 4 reviews the literature and identifies the gaps; Section 5 develops the theoretical framework; Section 6 presents the conceptual framework and the ten components of the SETILL model; Section 7 describes the design and methodology; Section 8 describes the case site; Section 9 states the expected contributions; Section 10 sets out the plan and timeline; Section 11 gives the preliminary chapter outline; and Section 12 lists the references. Appendices contain the draft instruments, the consent summary, a glossary and the reference verification record."),
}
